WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:07.045
So here we are, together.

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:10.549
But we're in a technological trance.

00:00:11.174 --> 00:00:13.885
Here we are together,
but we're divided by tribalism.

00:00:14.636 --> 00:00:18.098
Here we are together,
but we're distracted by mass myths.

00:00:18.515 --> 00:00:20.809
We've been separated by walls,

00:00:20.809 --> 00:00:23.728
windows, computer screens, borders.

00:00:24.396 --> 00:00:27.023
But we're united in our hearts.

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:30.402
Oh, we've argued
and quarreled in the past.

00:00:31.069 --> 00:00:33.822
But now we know we've got a common bond.

00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:34.990
Humanity.

00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:37.284
So why are we all here?

00:00:38.034 --> 00:00:39.995
Maybe we're all looking for a key.

00:00:40.453 --> 00:00:46.543
A key to unlock this political, economic,
social, cultural paralysis.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:51.423
Maybe the answer
is in a little word: empathy.

00:00:52.007 --> 00:00:56.052
Empathy is not just walking in someone's
shoes or feeling someone's pain.

00:00:56.094 --> 00:00:59.764
Empathy is the secret ingredient

00:00:59.806 --> 00:01:02.601
for finding a human connection.

00:01:03.226 --> 00:01:07.814
So I want you to open up your hearts
and open up your minds,

00:01:07.856 --> 00:01:09.733
because in times of confusion,

00:01:09.774 --> 00:01:15.113
we must not lose our capacity
to dare to be kind to each other.

00:01:16.364 --> 00:01:17.866
Some years ago,

00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:21.494
I had the great privilege
to be invited to the White House

00:01:21.536 --> 00:01:23.371
to do this.

00:01:24.581 --> 00:01:26.708
Now this has got nothing
to do with politics.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.961
This is a cultural moment
in American history.

00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:33.423
This is Michelle Obama's
first portrait as First Lady.

00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:39.095
(Applause)

00:01:39.137 --> 00:01:42.390
So on this day, I was nervous.

00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:45.810
And when I'm nervous,
I get socially clumsy.

00:01:45.852 --> 00:01:47.020
Know what I mean?

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.480
(Laughter)

00:01:48.521 --> 00:01:52.567
So I picked up my camera,
focused on her eyelash,

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:54.944
and in my clumsiness I shouted out,

00:01:54.944 --> 00:01:57.364
"Right, my love, I want your soul.

00:01:57.405 --> 00:01:58.573
Give it to me."

00:01:58.615 --> 00:02:00.659
(Laughter)

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.118
Silence in the room.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.495
(Laughter)

00:02:03.536 --> 00:02:06.998
I'm sure the hairdresser
almost dropped the hairspray in shock.

00:02:07.624 --> 00:02:08.958
And as I took this picture,

00:02:08.958 --> 00:02:11.419
this devastating look came over her face.

00:02:11.461 --> 00:02:13.546
The eyebrow raises as if to say,

00:02:13.546 --> 00:02:14.714
"How dare you?

00:02:14.756 --> 00:02:16.591
But I quite like it at the same time."

00:02:16.591 --> 00:02:18.385
(Laughter)

00:02:18.426 --> 00:02:20.512
So then I realize what I just said.

00:02:20.512 --> 00:02:23.682
And I said, "My goodness me,
I'm an Englishman.

00:02:23.723 --> 00:02:26.393
And in England we invented being polite.

00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:29.979
I think Canadians and Americans do it
better now, but we invented it.

00:02:30.021 --> 00:02:32.065
And here I am in the White House,

00:02:32.107 --> 00:02:34.609
calling you ‘my love’
and demanding your soul.

00:02:34.609 --> 00:02:36.611
Please forgive my atrocities."

00:02:37.487 --> 00:02:41.741
She stands up, gives me a hug
and whispers in my ear,

00:02:41.741 --> 00:02:46.287
"Platon, when all is said and done,
I'm just Michelle."

00:02:48.581 --> 00:02:51.334
This is Pussy Riot.

00:02:52.627 --> 00:02:56.339
You probably know them as the hardcore
feminist punk rock group

00:02:56.339 --> 00:03:00.385
who spoke truth to power
against Putin's excessive nationalism.

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:02.220
And consequently,

00:03:02.262 --> 00:03:05.807
they were sent to a Siberian
prison for two years.

00:03:06.558 --> 00:03:11.229
But what happens if we remove
those colorful, aggressive masks?

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.231
You see something different.

00:03:13.732 --> 00:03:16.401
This is Nadia and Masha

00:03:16.401 --> 00:03:19.195
photographed after
they were released from prison.

00:03:19.904 --> 00:03:23.324
Look at their faces, you can see
the vulnerability on their faces.

00:03:23.366 --> 00:03:25.910
You can see that they've
paid a heavy price

00:03:25.952 --> 00:03:28.580
for their support of LGBTQ rights

00:03:28.621 --> 00:03:30.457
and women's rights in Russia.

00:03:31.541 --> 00:03:37.213
Now during their trial
in the courtroom, before jail,

00:03:37.213 --> 00:03:41.009
they were kept in a big cage
as if they're wild animals.

00:03:42.427 --> 00:03:47.557
Nadia, the woman with dark hair,
the co-founder of Pussy Riot,

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:52.103
she was constantly scribbling handwritten
notes on a scrappy piece of paper.

00:03:52.771 --> 00:03:54.981
Eventually, the judge said to her,

00:03:55.023 --> 00:03:58.318
"I want you to stand up
and make your closing statement

00:03:58.318 --> 00:03:59.903
before I sentence you."

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.199
I'm sure Nadia must have known
she's about to go to jail.

00:04:05.074 --> 00:04:07.786
Nevertheless, she stood up.

00:04:08.495 --> 00:04:11.748
Nervously clutching her piece of paper,

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.168
took a deep breath
and read out from her notes.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.880
And what she said to the judge
and to the world, I believe,

00:04:18.922 --> 00:04:23.301
will go down as one
of our generation's greatest speeches.

00:04:23.301 --> 00:04:25.053
She said,

00:04:25.094 --> 00:04:27.597
"I wouldn't give people labels.

00:04:28.223 --> 00:04:30.558
There are no winners or losers here,

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.644
injured parties or accused.

00:04:32.644 --> 00:04:35.396
We just need to make contact,

00:04:35.396 --> 00:04:38.733
to establish a dialogue
and a joint search for truth,

00:04:38.775 --> 00:04:42.237
to seek wisdom together,
to be philosophers together,

00:04:42.278 --> 00:04:45.156
rather than stigmatizing
and labeling people.

00:04:45.198 --> 00:04:48.701
That is one of the worst things
people could do."

00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:54.290
I believe Nadia thinks
one of the worst things we could do

00:04:54.332 --> 00:04:56.000
is to judge each other.

00:04:56.626 --> 00:04:59.712
Maybe we need to be less judgmental

00:04:59.712 --> 00:05:01.089
and more curious.

00:05:01.756 --> 00:05:04.509
When you look at one of my pictures,

00:05:04.551 --> 00:05:08.137
you might find yourself making
judgments about that person.

00:05:08.972 --> 00:05:13.017
So what happens if I strip away
your capacity to make those judgments?

00:05:13.059 --> 00:05:15.854
I take away 95 percent of the picture,

00:05:15.895 --> 00:05:19.983
and I leave you with just five percent
that you can't recognize.

00:05:20.775 --> 00:05:22.986
But maybe it's the most important part.

00:05:23.403 --> 00:05:25.655
The window to someone's character.

00:05:25.697 --> 00:05:26.865
The eye.

00:05:28.157 --> 00:05:29.742
Whose eye is this?

00:05:29.742 --> 00:05:31.411
Always watching us.

00:05:32.078 --> 00:05:33.705
Is it someone you love?

00:05:33.746 --> 00:05:35.331
Is it someone you hate?

00:05:35.915 --> 00:05:38.376
It belongs to this man.

00:05:40.962 --> 00:05:42.839
This man.

00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:47.051
At this moment,

00:05:47.093 --> 00:05:52.390
I would like to pay tribute
to all the courageous, brave,

00:05:52.432 --> 00:05:54.267
resilient heroes

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:56.519
who fight against oppression,

00:05:56.519 --> 00:05:59.022
who fight for human rights,

00:05:59.063 --> 00:06:02.191
dignity, freedom and justice
around the world.

00:06:02.233 --> 00:06:04.569
Let's give them a round of applause.

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:05.737
Come on.

00:06:05.778 --> 00:06:12.744
(Applause)

00:06:12.952 --> 00:06:15.997
So I was driven
through the streets of Moscow,

00:06:15.997 --> 00:06:17.957
past the gates of the Kremlin,

00:06:17.999 --> 00:06:20.251
and we continued out
of the streets of Moscow

00:06:20.293 --> 00:06:23.004
into a dark, bleak, Gothic forest.

00:06:23.713 --> 00:06:28.092
We arrived at one of the most sinister
buildings I've ever seen in my life.

00:06:28.134 --> 00:06:30.845
His private residence, his private dacha.

00:06:31.346 --> 00:06:34.724
And it was surrounded
by a two-story high security wall

00:06:34.766 --> 00:06:37.352
with snipers all the way along the top.

00:06:37.352 --> 00:06:39.562
As I get out of the limo,

00:06:39.562 --> 00:06:42.774
I'm escorted into
the building at gunpoint.

00:06:43.733 --> 00:06:45.318
I'm led into a room.

00:06:46.361 --> 00:06:49.280
I waited, and he comes in
with a giant entourage.

00:06:49.322 --> 00:06:51.324
And I said, "Mr. President,

00:06:51.366 --> 00:06:54.744
before I capture this moment
of history on film,

00:06:54.786 --> 00:06:56.829
I've got a question I'd like to ask you."

00:06:57.455 --> 00:07:00.208
I said, "I was brought up
by my mom and dad

00:07:00.249 --> 00:07:02.877
listening to the music of The Beatles.

00:07:03.586 --> 00:07:06.547
I want to know if you
ever listened to The Beatles.”

00:07:07.006 --> 00:07:09.300
His two translators whisper in his ear.

00:07:09.676 --> 00:07:12.845
There's some confusing looks
amongst the entourage,

00:07:12.845 --> 00:07:14.555
and then his mood drops.

00:07:15.181 --> 00:07:17.850
And in Russian, he orders
the two translators

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:21.896
and all his political advisers
out of the room, immediately.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:24.482
The bodyguards stay.

00:07:25.441 --> 00:07:27.485
And then Putin turns to me

00:07:27.485 --> 00:07:30.530
and in perfect English, he says,

00:07:30.571 --> 00:07:31.948
“I love The Beatles.”

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.199
(Laughter)

00:07:33.241 --> 00:07:35.243
I said, "I didn't know you spoke English."

00:07:35.284 --> 00:07:36.995
He said, "I speak perfect English."

00:07:36.995 --> 00:07:39.414
I said, "In that case,
who's your favorite Beatle?"

00:07:39.455 --> 00:07:40.623
He said, "Paul."

00:07:40.623 --> 00:07:42.333
I said, "What's your favorite song?

00:07:42.333 --> 00:07:44.002
Is it 'Back in the USSR?'"

00:07:44.043 --> 00:07:46.295
(Laughter)

00:07:46.295 --> 00:07:48.089
Yeah, he didn't like that very much.

00:07:48.798 --> 00:07:50.508
And then he said, "No.

00:07:51.676 --> 00:07:54.137
My favorite song is ‘Yesterday.’

00:07:54.178 --> 00:07:55.430
Think about it."

00:07:56.305 --> 00:07:57.932
And I thought about it.

00:07:58.516 --> 00:08:02.353
And I realized I'm being sent
a subliminal message

00:08:02.395 --> 00:08:06.357
about the old days of power
and authority of the Soviet Union

00:08:06.357 --> 00:08:08.234
through a Paul McCartney song.

00:08:09.318 --> 00:08:14.657
Now, that human connection
I had with him allowed me in,

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:18.786
and I ended up about an inch and a half
away from his nose as I took this picture.

00:08:18.828 --> 00:08:22.331
I could feel his cold breath on my hand
as I focused the lens,

00:08:22.373 --> 00:08:24.375
and that's how I got the truth.

00:08:25.043 --> 00:08:29.589
The truth is that this is the cold face
of power and authority in Russia.

00:08:30.048 --> 00:08:33.718
And then he performs power
for me in his chair.

00:08:35.762 --> 00:08:40.308
Now the LGBTQ community
and the human rights community in Russia,

00:08:40.308 --> 00:08:44.353
many of their members
have adopted my pictures of Putin,

00:08:44.395 --> 00:08:46.105
and they used them as a banner

00:08:46.147 --> 00:08:50.943
to show everything that they believe
is wrong with power in their country.

00:08:50.985 --> 00:08:52.862
I've been told recently

00:08:52.904 --> 00:08:57.492
that anyone in Russia who is caught
circulating my pictures online

00:08:57.533 --> 00:09:00.161
in connection with human rights violations

00:09:00.203 --> 00:09:01.621
will be arrested

00:09:01.621 --> 00:09:04.082
and they will go to jail immediately.

00:09:07.001 --> 00:09:08.586
Whose eye is this?

00:09:09.128 --> 00:09:10.338
Someone you love?

00:09:10.379 --> 00:09:11.756
Someone you hate?

00:09:12.215 --> 00:09:16.636
It belongs to a man who was prepared
to go to jail for his beliefs.

00:09:17.178 --> 00:09:20.306
The late, great Muhammad Ali.

00:09:21.432 --> 00:09:25.895
I said to him, "Muhammad,
you are the greatest.

00:09:26.437 --> 00:09:28.106
Teach me to be great.

00:09:28.147 --> 00:09:30.233
How can my generation be as great

00:09:30.233 --> 00:09:33.694
as your generation had to be
during the civil rights era?"

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:37.281
He couldn't speak very well
because of Parkinson's.

00:09:37.615 --> 00:09:40.368
So I had to get close, and he whispered,

00:09:40.368 --> 00:09:42.245
"I have a confession to make."

00:09:42.286 --> 00:09:43.621
"What is it?" I said.

00:09:43.663 --> 00:09:46.499
He said, "I wasn't as great
as I said I was."

00:09:46.499 --> 00:09:47.708
(Laughter)

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:49.335
"Holy shit," I said.

00:09:49.377 --> 00:09:50.878
(Laughter)

00:09:50.878 --> 00:09:53.506
"That's the biggest confession
I ever heard in my life.

00:09:53.506 --> 00:09:56.134
The whole world knows
you as Ali, the greatest."

00:09:56.884 --> 00:09:58.511
And then he said,

00:09:58.553 --> 00:10:00.805
"I think you misunderstand me.

00:10:01.806 --> 00:10:04.016
I'll tell you what was great," he said.

00:10:04.433 --> 00:10:05.726
"It wasn't me.

00:10:06.185 --> 00:10:10.648
It was the people,
saw themselves in my struggle,

00:10:10.648 --> 00:10:12.108
in my story."

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.277
And then he turned it to me,

00:10:14.318 --> 00:10:18.281
And now I've got the great honor
to turn it to you.

00:10:18.948 --> 00:10:20.575
And he said,

00:10:20.616 --> 00:10:25.163
"If you can get people to see themselves
in the story that you put forward,

00:10:25.204 --> 00:10:27.456
then you have a chance
of achieving greatness.

00:10:27.498 --> 00:10:30.084
But that greatness is never
you personally.

00:10:30.459 --> 00:10:33.921
That's something much bigger,
called bridge building."

00:10:34.881 --> 00:10:38.634
A light bulb went off in my brain,
so I took this picture.

00:10:38.676 --> 00:10:41.137
My last ever portrait of Muhammad Ali.

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.598
Not as a prizefighter or a champion,

00:10:43.639 --> 00:10:45.558
but as a compassionate bridge builder,

00:10:45.558 --> 00:10:47.268
thinking of the underdog.

00:10:48.686 --> 00:10:50.104
Whose eye is this?

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.024
Someone you love, someone you hate?

00:10:53.649 --> 00:10:55.568
A different type of underdog.

00:10:56.235 --> 00:10:57.820
Belongs to this man.

00:10:58.738 --> 00:11:00.948
So I said to him, "Donald,

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:04.035
"Let's be human with each other
just for a few minutes.

00:11:04.076 --> 00:11:06.162
We've all followed your career," I said.

00:11:06.162 --> 00:11:10.416
"No one can doubt, it's an extraordinary
career path you've had.

00:11:10.458 --> 00:11:12.335
But there's always something about you.

00:11:13.002 --> 00:11:15.588
There's always an air
of tension and controversy

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.216
about things you say and do in public.

00:11:18.257 --> 00:11:21.177
And I'm sure that's
intentional on your part.

00:11:21.219 --> 00:11:24.764
But it feels to me as if you're
in the middle of an emotional storm.

00:11:24.764 --> 00:11:28.142
Personally, I couldn't live
with that anxiety all the time.

00:11:28.184 --> 00:11:30.519
I want to know how you weather the storm."

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:34.065
He calmly looks at me and he says,

00:11:34.065 --> 00:11:35.858
"I am the storm."

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:37.860
(Audience murmurs)

00:11:39.237 --> 00:11:42.406
The eyes are always watching us.

00:11:43.324 --> 00:11:46.244
But how do we use our eyes?

00:11:46.827 --> 00:11:47.954
Do we look?

00:11:48.788 --> 00:11:50.122
Or do we see?

00:11:51.457 --> 00:11:53.668
When you look at this picture,

00:11:53.709 --> 00:11:57.797
you might find yourself thinking
about the politics of war.

00:11:58.381 --> 00:12:01.300
But when you see
the person in this picture,

00:12:01.342 --> 00:12:03.010
you get to know Jessica.

00:12:03.886 --> 00:12:07.556
Jessica holds a flag they draped
over her husband's coffin,

00:12:07.598 --> 00:12:11.310
who had recently been killed
fighting in Iraq for America.

00:12:11.686 --> 00:12:13.604
I took this picture in her home.

00:12:14.563 --> 00:12:18.109
Now she has his wedding ring
around her neck on a chain.

00:12:18.901 --> 00:12:20.695
When you're killed in combat,

00:12:20.736 --> 00:12:23.656
the US military sends your clothes
and personal belongings

00:12:23.698 --> 00:12:26.117
back to the family in a big box.

00:12:26.534 --> 00:12:29.453
Jessica had recently received the box

00:12:29.453 --> 00:12:31.831
but had not yet had
the courage to open it.

00:12:32.456 --> 00:12:36.002
There it lay, closed, at the base
of her bed, in her bedroom.

00:12:37.044 --> 00:12:40.631
I said to her, "Jessica, maybe
you should wear one of his army T-shirts

00:12:40.673 --> 00:12:42.717
as a tribute to him in the picture."

00:12:42.758 --> 00:12:44.260
She said, "I would.

00:12:44.802 --> 00:12:47.513
But all his T-shirts are in the box.

00:12:48.347 --> 00:12:50.224
Maybe now," she said,

00:12:50.224 --> 00:12:52.893
"it's time for me to face that emotion."

00:12:54.061 --> 00:12:56.105
So we both walked into her bedroom.

00:12:56.772 --> 00:12:59.400
We both knelt at the base of her bed,

00:12:59.442 --> 00:13:03.029
and we undid a latch each
of the box and lifted the lid.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:05.781
And she burst into tears.

00:13:07.241 --> 00:13:09.994
And I felt ashamed of myself.

00:13:10.995 --> 00:13:12.663
For the first time in my life,

00:13:12.705 --> 00:13:16.334
I had put a photograph
above the well-being of a person.

00:13:17.043 --> 00:13:18.586
As I apologized, she said,

00:13:18.627 --> 00:13:21.380
"You don't understand why I'm crying.

00:13:22.173 --> 00:13:24.133
I'm crying," she said,

00:13:24.175 --> 00:13:27.178
“because I just realized
they washed his clothes,

00:13:27.219 --> 00:13:29.430
and I wanted to smell him again."

00:13:36.562 --> 00:13:38.022
Did you feel that?

00:13:41.734 --> 00:13:44.111
Did you feel that?

00:13:47.656 --> 00:13:49.533
If you felt that ...

00:13:51.702 --> 00:13:53.788
then something beautiful just happened.

00:13:55.456 --> 00:13:59.794
We all came into this room
defined by our differences.

00:14:00.252 --> 00:14:03.547
Men, women, old, young, Black, white.

00:14:03.589 --> 00:14:05.716
Dare I say, members of the Left.

00:14:06.008 --> 00:14:07.802
Dare I say, members of the Right.

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:09.762
Different genders,

00:14:09.804 --> 00:14:12.515
different religious groups, cultures.

00:14:12.515 --> 00:14:15.768
But we all felt that together.

00:14:16.143 --> 00:14:18.312
What we felt is compassion.

00:14:18.604 --> 00:14:21.232
Compassion for someone
you've never even met.

00:14:21.273 --> 00:14:23.818
That's the power of human connection.

00:14:23.859 --> 00:14:26.821
And that's why we will not go down

00:14:26.821 --> 00:14:31.117
as the generation of connectivity
that disconnected itself, no.

00:14:32.284 --> 00:14:33.869
I think we're going to rise up.

00:14:34.328 --> 00:14:37.498
I think we're going to rekindle
the spirit of optimism.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:42.920
It seems to me they underestimated
the resilience of humanity.

00:14:44.213 --> 00:14:45.673
Whose eye is this?

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:47.466
Someone you love?

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:49.176
Someone you hate?

00:14:49.844 --> 00:14:54.640
It belongs to a man who was robbed
of the power of human connection.

00:14:55.683 --> 00:14:57.309
Professor Stephen Hawking.

00:14:58.519 --> 00:15:00.980
Now, he was very ill
when I took this picture.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:04.024
In fact, his whole body
had essentially shut down.

00:15:04.024 --> 00:15:07.069
There was only one muscle
he could still work,

00:15:07.069 --> 00:15:09.363
and that was underneath his eye.

00:15:09.405 --> 00:15:13.951
His team of scientists placed a sensor
at the base of his glasses.

00:15:13.951 --> 00:15:15.453
You can see it here.

00:15:15.453 --> 00:15:18.205
And that sensor picks up
movement of that muscle.

00:15:18.247 --> 00:15:19.415
And with that muscle,

00:15:19.457 --> 00:15:22.293
he moves a cursor
over each letter of the alphabet

00:15:22.334 --> 00:15:23.878
on his laptop screen.

00:15:24.253 --> 00:15:26.881
He chooses a letter, builds a word,

00:15:26.922 --> 00:15:28.090
constructs a paragraph,

00:15:28.132 --> 00:15:32.511
and that's how he communicated
those epic statements about space,

00:15:32.553 --> 00:15:34.305
time and humanity to us.

00:15:35.514 --> 00:15:38.851
At the end of the session, I said to him,

00:15:38.893 --> 00:15:44.523
"Professor, what an honor
it is for me to photograph you.

00:15:44.523 --> 00:15:48.277
And I've seen how hard it is
for you to even communicate.

00:15:48.319 --> 00:15:50.821
But I'm going to ask for a big favor."

00:15:51.197 --> 00:15:54.033
I said, "I want one word
of wisdom from you.

00:15:54.366 --> 00:15:55.993
One word from you,

00:15:55.993 --> 00:15:58.913
it's going to be worth
a million words from anyone else."

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:00.789
We all waited.

00:16:01.123 --> 00:16:03.334
His nurse was a little concerned.

00:16:04.001 --> 00:16:06.837
I had heard that that muscle was failing,

00:16:06.879 --> 00:16:09.590
and most of the things
he typed around this time

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:11.300
didn't mean anything.

00:16:11.884 --> 00:16:15.221
Suddenly we heard, beep,

00:16:15.262 --> 00:16:17.598
beep ... beep, beep, beep.

00:16:18.432 --> 00:16:19.558
He was typing.

00:16:20.267 --> 00:16:25.231
We all leaned in to the laptop screen
to see where the cursor is going to go,

00:16:25.272 --> 00:16:28.317
and he went all the way to the letter W.

00:16:28.776 --> 00:16:30.736
And he starts the next letter,

00:16:30.778 --> 00:16:32.488
beep, beep, beep, beep, beep.

00:16:32.821 --> 00:16:34.698
All the way to the letter O.

00:16:35.449 --> 00:16:37.284
And then the final letter.

00:16:37.868 --> 00:16:39.787
Beep, beep, beep, beep, beep, beep.

00:16:40.454 --> 00:16:44.041
All the way back to the letter W.

00:16:44.542 --> 00:16:48.587
Someone on the team said,
"W-O-W what does that stand for?

00:16:48.629 --> 00:16:50.297
That's nonsense, it's a mistake."

00:16:51.048 --> 00:16:53.133
And I said, "Hold on a minute.

00:16:53.842 --> 00:16:55.803
That's not a mistake.

00:16:55.803 --> 00:16:57.930
He just typed the word, ‘Wow.’

00:16:58.305 --> 00:17:03.269
He just gave me a word my kids would use
about the beauty and majesty of life.

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:06.939
And isn't it amazing how the man
who faces so much adversity

00:17:06.981 --> 00:17:10.526
still looks at the world
through the eyes of wonder?"

00:17:11.151 --> 00:17:13.821
His nurse said, "He's exhausted.

00:17:14.238 --> 00:17:16.115
Let's give his eyes a rest.

00:17:16.115 --> 00:17:17.616
Let's take off his sensor,

00:17:17.658 --> 00:17:21.787
take off his glasses and replace them
with his dark sunglasses."

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:23.455
And then I saw it.

00:17:23.872 --> 00:17:27.001
A vision of a bad-ass rock star of hope.

00:17:27.042 --> 00:17:28.794
And I took this picture.

00:17:28.836 --> 00:17:32.715
This is my fitting tribute
to a legend of our time,

00:17:32.715 --> 00:17:36.719
a man who believed that connection
equals compassion.

00:17:37.845 --> 00:17:40.222
So I'm not going to leave you
with an answer.

00:17:41.056 --> 00:17:43.392
I'm going to leave you with a question.

00:17:44.643 --> 00:17:46.353
Are we to be bystanders,

00:17:46.395 --> 00:17:49.690
like moths dancing
around somebody else’s flame?

00:17:49.732 --> 00:17:51.984
Or are we to be upstanders,

00:17:52.026 --> 00:17:55.946
and we light darkness ourselves
with our own torch of compassion?

00:17:55.946 --> 00:17:58.157
Because when there is light,

00:17:58.198 --> 00:18:02.578
we see each other not as strangers,

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:04.371
not as opponents

00:18:04.413 --> 00:18:06.790
but as creative partners.

00:18:06.832 --> 00:18:10.502
And then together,
we rebuild this troubled world.

00:18:10.878 --> 00:18:12.838
Together, we reconnect.

00:18:12.838 --> 00:18:16.091
Together, we earn the respect of history

00:18:16.133 --> 00:18:21.430
and transform this chaos
into a beautiful cosmos.

00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:22.931
Thank you for listening to me.

00:18:22.973 --> 00:18:29.730
(Applause)

